SHA1: e1c45a4c528ae7b7f7757c847526fe3f33032dc5
SHA256: 5f2206abccac6981082a257c13ebbc59aa7b3b09a5953e609f251f917b3fe0a8
Section: admin
Priority: optional
Description: CanLinux OS 1.0 generic laptop Fn / function-key support
 Hardware-independent keyboard-backlight support for every CanLinux / Fihri OS 1.0
 laptop (Intel, AMD, ACPI, gmux, Dell, ASUS, ThinkPad, Framework, Apple SMC, ...).
  1. /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/90-canlinux-backlight.rules grants the desktop
     "video" group WRITE access (mode 0660 root:video, no world access) to the
     brightness attributes of EVERY /sys/class/backlight device and of EVERY
     *kbd_backlight* LED.  The rule matches ACTION=="add" only, so it can never
     re-grant a permission that the RDP privacy lock has just revoked.
  2. /usr/local/bin/canlinux-kbd-backlight discovers any *kbd_backlight* LED,
     applies a bounded +/-25 step clamped to the device's own max_brightness
     and falls back through the DDE BacklightHelper1, direct sysfs and UPower
     backends.  It is a silent no-op on machines without a keyboard backlight.
  3. A GSettings override clears the three DEAD media-key grabs
     (kbd-brightness-up / kbd-brightness-down / kbd-light-on-off) of
     dde-session-daemon 6.0.43, whose KbdLightController constructor is absent
     from the shipped binary, so that the custom shortcut can grab the keys.
     The working panel-brightness (mon-brightness-*) and all audio/media
     bindings are left untouched.
  4. /usr/share/canlinux-laptop-hotkeys/custom.ini is the SHIPPED shortcut
     fragment; it is merged into /etc/skel/.config/deepin/dde-daemon/keybinding/
     custom.ini and into every human user's
     ~/.config/deepin/dde-daemon/keybinding/custom.ini and binds
     XF86KbdBrightnessUp / XF86KbdBrightnessDown / XF86KbdLightOnOff to the
     helper.  The postinst verifies the merge and fails loudly when the
     fragment is missing or the merge did not take effect.
 This package changes NO keybinding of the panel brightness, the volume or the
 media keys, touches NO service, does NOT replace or restart
 canlinux-session-owner, and applies NO Apple-specific remap: Mission Control
 (XF86LaunchA) and Launchpad (XF86LaunchB) stay scoped to the hardware-gated
 fihri-macbook-support package.

